abstract: >-
  Mapping Voices is an open digital-humanities research infrastructure that
  makes geographically and linguistically dispersed oral-history and
  voice-testimony collections discoverable. Its dataset describes real,
  publicly documented collections with persistent identifiers, controlled
  geographic, linguistic, and thematic metadata, verification status, and
  provenance, and links each to its holding institution's own page. It is
  published as JSON, CSV, and GeoJSON with a documented methodology. Counts
  describe the indexed collections, not the global distribution of oral
  history. The application source code is MIT-licensed; the dataset is
  licensed under CC BY 4.0 — see LICENSE.
